<speak>
<p>Witness. Field Season One. Chapter Five: The Voice Returns to the Forest. The alala, Hawaii's crow.</p>
<break time="800ms"/>
<p>There is a category of endangered animal sadder than critically endangered. It is called extinct in the wild — a species that still exists, but only behind fences. Since 2002, the alala, Hawaii's crow, has lived there: roughly one hundred and ten to one hundred and twenty birds in two conservation centers. And none at all in the forests that shaped them.</p>
<p>This chapter is about the day that stopped being the whole story. In November 2024, five young alala stepped through an open aviary door on the slopes of Haleakala, on Maui. And as of January 2026, more than a year later, all five are still out there. Alive, and free. One pair has already built a nest and laid an egg. The people who raised them wept at the door. So might you.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The past. How the forest lost its voice.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>The alala is a crow the way Hawaii is an island: the same idea, transformed into something found nowhere else. Larger than mainland crows, with bristly feathers about the bill, it mates for life and speaks in dozens of calls — sweet chirps, rhythmic whoops, cries that visitors describe as eerily human. In Hawaiian tradition the alala is aumakua — an ancestral guardian — and it appears in the Kumulipo, the creation chant. As one of its keepers puts it: these birds, they are our ancestors.</p>
<p>The forest needed the bird as much as the culture did. Alala swallow and spread the seeds of more than thirty native plants — for some, they were the only courier. Then the threats arrived in layers: forests cleared for cattle and cane. Rats, cats, and mongoose finding the nests. Mosquitoes carrying avian malaria upslope. And the io, the native Hawaiian hawk, picking off young birds that no longer had elders to warn them.</p>
<p>Seventy-six birds in 1976. Thirteen by 1992. In 2002, the last wild pair was seen, and the wild story ended. Two rescue attempts failed painfully: twenty-seven birds released in the 1990s — twenty-one died. About thirty more between 2016 and 2020 — roughly twenty-five died, many taken by hawks, and the five survivors were brought back inside.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The present. Five birds on a new island.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>The program learned from every loss. And then it changed the map. Maui has no io at all. In November 2024, five juveniles — hatched in conservation care, schooled to fear predators using a taxidermy hawk with flapping wings, named by children from five Maui schools — were released into the Kipahulu Forest Reserve, carrying the first GPS backpacks ever worn by their species.</p>
<p>Everything before this said they would die. Instead: after five months, all five were foraging on native fruits, riding out storms, expanding their vocabulary. After a year — all five, still. In late 2025, two of them paired, built a nest, and produced an egg. The female sat the full three weeks of incubation. First eggs rarely hatch, and this one's fate is unknown. But a species that was extinct in the wild is nesting in it again. Two more birds have been staged for release to join them.</p>
<p>What is known: the five have outlived every previous release's trajectory. The trained fear of hawks held in the real forest. And the whole species can use stick tools — one of only two tool-using crows on Earth.</p>
<p>What is unknown: whether the pairs will raise chicks. And the hard ceiling underneath everything — every living alala descends from just nine founders, and more than half of the program's eggs fail to hatch. A burden written in the genes, that no single good year can lift.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>The future. What would improve its odds.</p>
<break time="600ms"/>
<p>One. Grow the Maui flock. Each new release into hawk-free Kipahulu tests and teaches; the birds' own culture — warning calls, foraging knowledge — rebuilds only in the wild.</p>
<p>Two. Take back the mosquitoes. A coalition is dropping roughly half a million lab-reared male mosquitoes per week over East Maui's forests — males that father no offspring — to crash the population carrying avian malaria.</p>
<p>Three. Manage nine founders' worth of genes. Genome-guided pairing is the program's quiet daily labor against the hatch-failure ceiling.</p>
<p>Four. Go home, eventually. Maui is a stepping stone. The long plan returns the alala to Hawaii Island itself, once the hawk problem has an answer.</p>
<p>In the deep past, says one of the recovery workers, people picked out that voice from all the voices of the forest. We can't lose that voice. For the first time in a generation, on one mountain, no one has to.</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>One question to carry with you. The alala's keepers spent twenty years failing in public, and trying again — and the fifth attempt is flying. What have you stopped attempting, after two failures?</p>
<break time="1s"/>
<p>Every claim in this chapter is backed by a dated source, listed in the chapter text. This narration uses a synthetic voice. Thank you for witnessing.</p>
</speak>
